Network Devices & Local Area Network (LAN)
Introduction
A network device is a hardware component used to connect computers, servers, printers, and other electronic devices to form a communication network. These devices enable communication by transmitting, receiving, routing, and managing data across the network.


1. Network Interface Card (NIC)
Definition
A Network Interface Card (NIC) is a hardware component that allows a computer to connect to a wired or wireless network.
Features
•	Operates at Layer 1 (Physical) and Layer 2 (Data Link). 
•	Has a unique 48-bit MAC Address assigned by the manufacturer. 
•	Available in Ethernet (wired) and Wi-Fi (wireless) versions. 
•	Converts digital data into electrical, optical, or wireless signals. 
Advantages
•	Enables communication between computers. 
•	Provides high-speed network connectivity. 
•	Supports both wired and wireless networking. 
•	Does not reduce network congestion. 
 
2. Repeater
Definition
A Repeater is a networking device that regenerates weak signals and retransmits them over longer distances.
Features
•	Operates at Layer 1 (Physical). 
•	Regenerates weak signals. 
•	Does not inspect data or addresses. 
•	Usually has two ports. 
Advantages
•	Extends network coverage. 
•	Improves signal quality. 
Disadvantages
•	Cannot filter traffic. 
•	Does not reduce network congestion
. 
3. Hub
Definition
A Hub is a multi-port repeater that broadcasts incoming data to all connected devices.
Features
•	Operates at OSI Layer 1 (Physical Layer) 
•	Broadcasts data to every connected device 
•	Single collision domain 
•	Single broadcast domain 
•	Supports half-duplex communication 
Advantages
•	Low cost 
•	Easy to install 
•	Simple to use 
Disadvantages
•	Causes frequent network collisions 
•	Low performance 
•	No security or traffic filtering 
 
________________________________________
4. Bridge
Definition
A Bridge connects two LAN segments and forwards frames using MAC addresses.
Features
•	Operates at OSI Layer 2 (Data Link Layer) 
•	Learns MAC addresses 
•	Filters unnecessary traffic 
•	Reduces collisions 
•	Connects two LAN segments 
Advantages
•	Improves network performance 
•	Reduces unnecessary traffic 
•	Decreases collisions 
Disadvantages
•	Supports only a limited number of LAN segments 
•	Slower than modern switches 
•	Becoming less common  
________________________________________
5. Switch
Definition
A Switch is a multi-port bridge that forwards data only to the destination device using MAC addresses.
Features
•	Operates at OSI Layer 2 (Data Link Layer) 
•	Uses a MAC Address (CAM) Table 
•	Supports full-duplex communication 
•	Each port has its own collision domain 
•	Single broadcast domain (unless VLANs are configured) 
Types of Switches
•	Unmanaged Switch 
•	Managed Switch 
•	PoE (Power over Ethernet) Switch 
•	Layer 3 Switch 
Advantages
•	High-speed communication 
•	Reduces collisions 
•	Efficient bandwidth utilization 
•	Improves network performance 
Disadvantages
•	More expensive than hubs 
•	Managed switches require configuration 
•	Broadcast traffic still exists without VLANs  
________________________________________

6. Router
Definition
A Router connects different networks and forwards packets using IP addresses.
Features
•	Operates at OSI Layer 3 (Network Layer) 
•	Uses IP addresses for routing 
•	Maintains routing tables 
•	Performs Network Address Translation (NAT) 
•	Connects LANs to WANs or the Internet 
Advantages
•	Connects multiple networks 
•	Separates broadcast domains 
•	Provides Internet connectivity 
•	Supports security features like ACLs and firewalls 
Disadvantages
•	Higher cost 
•	More complex configuration 
•	Slower than switches for local traffic  
________________________________________
7. Gateway
Definition
A Gateway connects networks using different communication protocols and acts as a protocol translator.
Features
•	Operates across multiple OSI layers (commonly Layer 7) 
•	Performs protocol conversion 
•	Connects different network architectures 
•	Often integrated with routers 
Advantages
•	Enables communication between different protocols 
•	Supports protocol translation 
•	Allows interoperability between dissimilar networks 
Disadvantages
•	Expensive 
•	Complex to configure and maintain 
•	 
