VPN – Virtual Private Network
 
Definition:
VPN is a technology that creates a secure and encrypted connection over a public network such as the Internet.
Characteristics
•	Provides encrypted communication. 
•	Allows secure remote access to a private network. 
•	Can protect data from unauthorized access. 
•	Uses VPN protocols to establish a secure connection. 
•	Can connect users to a network from different locations. 
Advantages
•	Improves privacy and security. 
•	Protects data while using public networks. 
•	Allows employees to securely access office resources remotely. 
•	Useful for remote working. 
•	Can help protect sensitive information from interception. 
Disadvantages
•	May reduce Internet speed because of encryption and routing. 
•	Some VPN services require payment. 
•	Configuration can be complicated for beginners. 
•	The VPN provider may potentially see some connection information. 
•	A VPN does not protect against every type of cyberattack. 
________________________________________
Wi-Fi – Wireless Fidelity
 
Definition:
Wi-Fi is a wireless networking technology based on IEEE 802.11 standards that allows devices to connect to a network and access the Internet using radio waves.
Characteristics
•	Uses radio waves for communication. 
•	Provides wireless Internet and network access. 
•	Commonly uses a wireless router or access point. 
•	Supports laptops, smartphones, tablets, smart TVs, and IoT devices. 
•	Can support multiple devices simultaneously. 
Advantages
•	No cables are required for connecting devices. 
•	Easy and convenient to use. 
•	Allows mobility within the coverage area. 
•	Easy to connect multiple devices. 
•	Widely available in homes, offices, colleges, airports, and public places. 
Disadvantages
•	Limited coverage range. 
•	Can suffer from interference. 
•	Wireless networks require proper security configuration. 
•	Speed can decrease when many users share the network. 
•	Physical obstacles such as walls can weaken signals. 
