 GAN – Global Area Network
 
Definition:
GAN is a network that connects computers and networks across very large geographical areas, potentially worldwide.
Characteristics
•	Covers countries, continents, or the entire world. 
•	Connects multiple WANs and other networks. 
•	Uses technologies such as fiber-optic links, satellites, and undersea cables. 
•	Supports global communication and data sharing. 
•	Requires large-scale network infrastructure. 
Advantages
•	Enables worldwide communication. 
•	Allows organizations to connect offices in different countries. 
•	Supports global data and resource sharing. 
•	Makes international services possible. 
•	Provides access to services from different geographical locations. 
Disadvantages
•	Very expensive to establish and maintain. 
•	Complex to manage. 
•	Security risks are higher because of its large scale. 
•	Network failures can affect communication across large areas. 
•	Performance depends on long-distance connections and network infrastructure. 

